{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"04ab22cb-6b34-48ab-9854-692b0e64b54c","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A cubic can have two turning points. We’ll locate them for this curve, then decide which is a local maximum and which is a local minimum.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Local maxima and minima"},{"op":"equation","id":"function","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f(x)=x^3-3x"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.2,2.2],"y":[-4.5,4.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"f(x)"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"cubic","y":"x^3-3*x","domain":[-2.2,2.2]}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"04ab22cb-6b34-48ab-9854-692b0e64b54c","step":{"id":"s2","script":"At a smooth turning point, the tangent is horizontal. Set the derivative equal to zero to find the two candidates.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"derivative","anchor":{"el":"function","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"f'(x)=3x^2-3=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=\\pm1"},{"op":"figure","id":"candidates","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"peak","at":{"on":"cubic","x":-1}},{"kind":"point","id":"valley","at":{"on":"cubic","x":1}},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":-1,"length":0.28,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"tangent","to":"cubic","x":1,"length":0.28,"stroke":"dashed"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"04ab22cb-6b34-48ab-9854-692b0e64b54c","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The derivative is positive outside these candidates and negative between them. At minus one, the curve changes from increasing to decreasing: a local maximum.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"signs","anchor":{"el":"derivative","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"f'(x):\\quad +\\ \\big|_{-1}\\ -\\ \\big|_{1}\\ +"},{"op":"figure","id":"maximum","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[-1,2.8],"text":"max (−1, 2)"},{"kind":"segment","from":[-1.85,-1.8],"to":[-1.55,0.1],"arrow":true},{"kind":"segment","from":[-0.4,1.6],"to":[0.1,0.1],"arrow":true}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At x = 1, the derivative changes from negative to positive. What kind of extremum occurs there?","expected_gist":"A local minimum, because the function changes from decreasing to increasing."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"04ab22cb-6b34-48ab-9854-692b0e64b54c","step":{"id":"s4","script":"At one, decreasing becomes increasing, giving a local minimum. These extrema compare nearby values; this cubic has no absolute maximum or minimum over all real numbers.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"minimum","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1,-2.8],"text":"min (1, −2)"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.55,-0.1],"to":[1.85,1.8],"arrow":true}]},{"op":"equation","id":"results","anchor":{"el":"signs","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\text{Local max: }f(-1)=2\\qquad\\text{Local min: }f(1)=-2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
